WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.192
In 1995, NASA dropped a machine into
Jupiter.

00:00:04.552 --> 00:00:08.090
It lasted fifty-eight minutes before the
planet silenced it.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.609
And what it sent back made no sense.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:12.509
The air was bone dry.

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:15.887
And something down there had stolen the
neon.

00:00:16.247 --> 00:00:18.270
Nothing has gone deeper since.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.696
Until her.

00:00:20.406 --> 00:00:23.507
Vesper to relay, entry in ten seconds.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.967
Noor, sweetheart, if you're watching this,
I'll be home before your birthday.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:29.381
I promise.

00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:32.736
These are the recordings she sent back.

00:00:34.236 --> 00:00:39.098
How do you survive hitting a planet at a
hundred and seventy thousand kilometres an

00:00:39.098 --> 00:00:39.391
hour?

00:00:39.751 --> 00:00:42.007
The real probe did it with brute force.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:47.504
A heat shield designed to burn away as it
fell, and braking so violent it felt two

00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:50.386
hundred and twenty-eight times its own
weight.

00:00:50.746 --> 00:00:52.171
A human can't take that.

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:58.735
In 1954, a test pilot survived about
forty-six g, and it nearly blinded him.

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:00.891
So Mara's capsule cheats.

00:01:01.251 --> 00:01:06.703
It skims the edge of the atmosphere at a
shallow angle, braking for minutes instead

00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:07.426
of seconds.

00:01:08.136 --> 00:01:09.105
Contact!

00:01:09.465 --> 00:01:13.115
The shield is burning, there's fire on
every window!

00:01:13.475 --> 00:01:17.344
Six g, seven, eight, I can't breathe, I
can't...

00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:20.538
Outside, the gas turns to plasma.

00:01:20.898 --> 00:01:22.706
Sixteen thousand degrees.

00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:26.526
Almost three times hotter than the surface
of the Sun.

00:01:27.236 --> 00:01:28.147
Still here.

00:01:28.507 --> 00:01:29.502
I'm still here.

00:01:29.862 --> 00:01:31.213
The parachute is out.

00:01:31.923 --> 00:01:34.138
Mara has survived the easy part.

00:01:35.638 --> 00:01:38.607
Noor, you should see this.

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.556
Clouds of ice, like snow that never lands.

00:01:43.266 --> 00:01:45.432
She's falling through Jupiter's weather.

00:01:45.792 --> 00:01:49.838
Three decks of cloud, stacked about
seventy-one kilometres deep.

00:01:50.198 --> 00:01:54.589
Ammonia ice on top, then ammonium
hydrosulfide, then water.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:57.165
Something's flashing below me.

00:01:57.525 --> 00:02:00.526
It's lightning, and it's lighting up the
whole deck.

00:02:01.236 --> 00:02:05.816
Juno watched these storms throw out hail
made of ammonia and water slush.

00:02:06.176 --> 00:02:08.587
Scientists call them mushballs.

00:02:09.297 --> 00:02:10.592
Wind!

00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:13.557
Hold on, hold on!

00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:18.855
Six hundred and forty metres per second!

00:02:19.565 --> 00:02:21.278
That's what the real probe measured.

00:02:21.638 --> 00:02:24.040
Almost twice the speed of sound.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.602
And then, Mara's instruments find the
first clue.

00:02:29.312 --> 00:02:31.596
Humidity is dropping.

00:02:31.956 --> 00:02:34.849
It's dry down here, completely dry.

00:02:35.559 --> 00:02:38.752
The real probe found the same thing in
1995.

00:02:39.112 --> 00:02:42.612
Almost no water, in a planet that should
be full of it.

00:02:42.972 --> 00:02:44.752
For years it was a puzzle.

00:02:45.112 --> 00:02:47.714
The answer turned out to be bad luck.

00:02:48.074 --> 00:02:54.025
The probe had fallen into a rare desert in
the sky, a hot spot where dry air sinks.

00:02:54.385 --> 00:02:55.819
But the missing neon?

00:02:56.179 --> 00:03:00.872
That clue is still waiting, thousands of
kilometres further down.

00:03:02.372 --> 00:03:06.853
About an hour after entry, Mara reaches a
place no one has ever passed.

00:03:07.213 --> 00:03:11.627
In 1995, the Galileo probe went silent
right here.

00:03:11.987 --> 00:03:13.925
A hundred and eighty kilometres down.

00:03:14.285 --> 00:03:17.081
Twenty-two times the pressure of Earth's
air.

00:03:17.441 --> 00:03:19.167
Hotter than boiling water.

00:03:19.877 --> 00:03:22.021
Passing the depth where Galileo died.

00:03:22.381 --> 00:03:24.007
I wanted to say something.

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:25.093
Thank you.

00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:27.017
You showed us the way down.

00:03:27.377 --> 00:03:31.894
From now on, nobody has ever seen this,
and I'm the first person to see it.

00:03:32.604 --> 00:03:35.996
Below this point, everything we know comes
from the outside.

00:03:36.356 --> 00:03:42.329
From Juno, circling the planet since 2016,
weighing it with gravity and listening to

00:03:42.329 --> 00:03:43.538
it in microwaves.

00:03:45.038 --> 00:03:49.131
I'm two hundred kilometres down, and the
wind still hasn't stopped.

00:03:49.841 --> 00:03:50.562
It won't.

00:03:50.922 --> 00:03:56.376
Juno found that Jupiter's stripes are jet
streams that reach about three thousand

00:03:56.376 --> 00:03:57.453
kilometres deep.

00:03:57.813 --> 00:04:03.074
And the Great Red Spot, a storm twice as
wide as Earth, has roots about five

00:04:03.074 --> 00:04:04.736
hundred kilometres down.

00:04:05.096 --> 00:04:09.125
The probe's whole journey would fit inside
it almost three times.

00:04:09.485 --> 00:04:12.393
But deeper still, something stranger is
waiting.

00:04:13.893 --> 00:04:15.549
Pressure warning!

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:19.311
The hull is groaning, that's not a sound I
want to hear.

00:04:19.671 --> 00:04:23.134
The outside looks thick and heavy, like
we're under water.

00:04:23.844 --> 00:04:25.046
There's no splash.

00:04:25.406 --> 00:04:26.385
No surface.

00:04:26.745 --> 00:04:31.987
Hydrogen, almost ninety percent of
Jupiter, is squeezed until it slowly turns

00:04:31.987 --> 00:04:33.417
from gas into liquid.

00:04:33.777 --> 00:04:35.439
Mara isn't falling anymore.

00:04:35.799 --> 00:04:36.908
She's sinking.

00:04:37.618 --> 00:04:39.451
Noor, I'm in an ocean.

00:04:39.811 --> 00:04:44.324
An ocean bigger than Earth, and I'm the
only one in it.

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:49.017
Ten thousand kilometres down, the second
clue appears.

00:04:49.727 --> 00:04:52.084
Something is falling past me.

00:04:52.444 --> 00:04:54.166
Drops, silver drops.

00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:55.900
That's not water.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:57.664
It's helium.

00:04:58.374 --> 00:05:01.310
This is our best understanding of the
physics.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:06.886
At a million times Earth's air pressure
and around five thousand degrees, helium

00:05:06.886 --> 00:05:10.211
may stop mixing with the hydrogen and fall
as rain.

00:05:10.571 --> 00:05:14.246
And as it falls, the helium rain drags
neon down with it.

00:05:14.606 --> 00:05:18.351
Remember the stolen neon the probe found
in 1995?

00:05:18.711 --> 00:05:21.584
Only a tenth of what the Sun has.

00:05:21.944 --> 00:05:23.884
This may be the thief.

00:05:25.384 --> 00:05:30.079
Halfway to the centre, the pressure rips
electrons off the hydrogen atoms.

00:05:30.439 --> 00:05:34.150
The ocean starts to conduct electricity,
like liquid metal.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.978
Every compass on board is spinning!

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:40.647
There's current everywhere!

00:05:41.357 --> 00:05:46.931
This churning metal ocean, inside a planet
that spins once every ten hours, is a

00:05:46.931 --> 00:05:47.837
giant dynamo.

00:05:48.197 --> 00:05:53.167
It powers a magnetic field up to
fifty-four times stronger than Earth's.

00:05:53.877 --> 00:05:55.889
Relay link is failing.

00:05:56.249 --> 00:05:58.622
I don't know if you're still getting this.

00:06:00.122 --> 00:06:04.398
For decades, textbooks drew a small, solid
rock at Jupiter's centre.

00:06:04.758 --> 00:06:07.977
Juno's gravity data says that picture is
wrong.

00:06:08.337 --> 00:06:10.442
The core is huge and fuzzy.

00:06:10.802 --> 00:06:15.406
Partly dissolved into the metal around it,
maybe stretching more than halfway to the

00:06:15.406 --> 00:06:16.009
cloud tops.

00:06:16.369 --> 00:06:21.401
One idea: when Jupiter was young, a planet
about ten times the mass of Earth may have

00:06:21.401 --> 00:06:23.651
hit it head-on and shattered its core.

00:06:24.011 --> 00:06:25.610
It never settled again.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.913
There's no floor and no rock, it just
keeps getting hotter.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:34.967
Near the centre, scientists estimate
around twenty-four thousand degrees.

00:06:35.327 --> 00:06:38.876
More than four times hotter than the
surface of the Sun.

00:06:39.586 --> 00:06:41.962
Noor, I'm not going to make your birthday.

00:06:42.322 --> 00:06:43.437
I'm so sorry.

00:06:43.797 --> 00:06:47.326
But I saw the heart of a giant, and it was
beautiful.

00:06:48.826 --> 00:06:50.745
Mara's journey was imagined.

00:06:51.105 --> 00:06:52.448
The probe's was not.

00:06:52.808 --> 00:06:58.924
In 1995, a small machine really did fall
into Jupiter, and for fifty-eight minutes

00:06:58.924 --> 00:07:00.788
it told us what it found.

00:07:01.148 --> 00:07:02.727
A desert in the sky.

00:07:03.087 --> 00:07:04.664
Missing neon.

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:07.383
Clues we are still following today.

00:07:07.743 --> 00:07:10.791
Every fact about Jupiter in this story is
real.

00:07:11.151 --> 00:07:15.885
Everything below that probe's grave, we've
only ever seen from far outside.

00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:19.854
If you want to fall into another world
with us, subscribe.
